1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,150
Think of a moment you remember.

2
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:06,490
A place.

3
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:07,590
The light.

4
00:00:08,210 --> 00:00:09,390
The people who were there.

5
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:12,390
It felt solid.

6
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:13,430
Real.

7
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:17,210
And then, it was gone.

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:20,830
Does the past still exist?

9
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:27,380
We live inside a single moving instant.

10
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:30,490
Each moment arrives, and slips behind

11
00:00:30,490 --> 00:00:33,000
us, like the walls of an endless tunnel.

12
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:36,990
We call that motion time.

13
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:38,380
And what it leaves

14
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:40,290
behind, we call the past.

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,190
But where does it go?

16
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:49,090
We keep pieces of it in memory.

17
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:51,850
Yet memories are not recordings.

18
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:55,390
Each time we remember, the mind rebuilds

19
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:57,150
the scene, a little differently.

20
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:00,270
Fragments fade.

21
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:01,620
Edges blur.

22
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,040
And yet, the universe

23
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:08,210
keeps its own records.

24
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,650
The sunlight on your skin left

25
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:13,130
the Sun eight minutes ago.

26
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:15,890
The light of the Andromeda galaxy set

27
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:18,000
out two and a half million years ago.

28
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:21,980
To look out is to look back.

29
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,460
The past is still arriving.

30
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:26,690
Physics goes further.

31
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:30,550
In Einstein's relativity, there

32
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:33,070
is no single, universal now.

33
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:35,830
What is now for one observer can

34
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:38,130
be past, or future, for another.

35
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:41,880
So some physicists picture

36
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:45,030
spacetime as a block: past, present

37
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:47,510
and future, all laid out together.

38
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,520
Nothing disappears.

39
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,060
Every moment simply is.

40
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:56,150
When his friend Michele Besso died,

41
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,250
Einstein wrote that the distinction

42
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:01,190
between past, present and future is

43
00:02:01,530 --> 00:02:03,560
only a stubbornly persistent illusion.

44
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:08,710
But not everyone agrees.

45
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:13,050
Perhaps only the present is real, and

46
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,520
the past survives only in its traces.

47
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:19,630
Perhaps the past is fixed, and

48
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:21,260
the future is still being written.

49
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:25,840
Or perhaps every moment exists, and

50
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:28,520
the passing is something we feel.

51
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Physics has not decided.

52
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:35,440
The moment you just lived

53
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,690
is already behind you.

54
00:02:37,410 --> 00:02:39,010
Its light is still travelling.

55
00:02:39,570 --> 00:02:41,040
Its traces are scattered

56
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:41,720
through the world.

57
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,680
So, if the past no longer exists...

58
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:49,780
Where did it go?
